         <head>
            <ce:title id="title0010">Reappraisal of negative pictures: a synthetic fixture</ce:title>
            <ce:abstract xml:lang="en" id="abs0010" view="all" class="author"><ce:section-title id="st0010">Abstract</ce:section-title><ce:abstract-sec id="abssec0010" view="all">
                  <ce:simple-para id="abspara0010" view="all">Twenty adults reappraised negative pictures during fMRI.</ce:simple-para>
               </ce:abstract-sec></ce:abstract>
         </head>
         <body view="all">
            <ce:sections>
               <ce:section id="sec1" view="all">
                  <ce:label>1</ce:label>
                  <ce:section-title id="sectitle0010">Methods</ce:section-title>
                  <ce:para id="p0010" view="all">Twenty healthy adults viewed negative and neutral pictures from the International Affective Picture System. On reappraise trials they reinterpreted the scene to feel less negative; on look trials they viewed it naturally without trying to change their response. Each trial showed the instruction for two seconds, the picture for seven seconds, and a rating scale for four seconds, followed by a jittered fixation interval. Participants practised the strategy outside the scanner until they could describe their reappraisals aloud.</ce:para>
                  <ce:para id="p0015" view="all">Images were acquired on a 3 T scanner with a whole-brain echo-planar sequence. Preprocessing included slice-timing correction, realignment, normalisation to MNI space and smoothing with an 8 mm kernel. First-level models included one regressor per condition and six motion parameters. The contrast reappraise negative &gt; look negative was taken to a one-sample t-test at the group level, thresholded at p &lt; 0.001 uncorrected with an extent of ten voxels, and reported without any region-of-interest restriction.</ce:para>
               </ce:section>
               <ce:section id="sec2" view="all">
                  <ce:label>2</ce:label>
                  <ce:section-title id="sectitle0015">Results</ce:section-title>
                  <ce:para id="p0020" view="all">Reappraisal reduced negative ratings relative to looking. At the neural level, reappraise &gt; look engaged the left inferior frontal gyrus, and the right amygdala was more active for look than for reappraise in a separate test (<ce:float-anchor refid="tbl1"/>). These effects were present in every participant who completed the practice session, and the size of the rating change correlated with the frontal response across participants. We report all peaks surviving the whole-brain threshold in the table below, including those outside the regions of prior interest.</ce:para>
                  <ce:para id="p0025" view="all">Behavioural effects of reappraisal were robust. Ratings after reappraise trials were lower than after look trials for negative pictures, with no difference for neutral pictures. Reaction times did not differ between conditions, suggesting that the instruction did not change how quickly participants responded to the rating scale. Together these findings replicate the canonical reappraisal effect in a new sample.</ce:para>
               </ce:section>
            </ce:sections>
         </body>
